course: everyday
lesson: 430
title: Lies that grow: one lie needs three more
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Noah in Boston wanted to sound wicked cool — in Boston «wicked» means «very» — so he told his mates his uncle played for the Red Sox. They asked for an autograph. So the uncle was «injured». They asked when he'd be back. «Secret mission.» Then a mate checked the team list online: no uncle. For the rest of the year the class called him «Secret Mission».
explain: That is how a lie grows. Every lie needs a bodyguard of new lies, because people ask the next question. Freya said she was at Amy's house; Mum rang Amy's mum, so it was Amy's gran's; which gran? «The other one.» Freya was really at the park after dark, and Mum drove there and found her. «One week grounded for the park, one for the lies.»
explain: Jake in Manchester swapped his bus pass for sweets and told Mum he'd lost it. Where? «On the bus.» Which bus? «The 86.» Nothing was handed in — and then Mum saw a girl from his class using a pass with Jake's photo on it. «Our kid,» said Mum, «you dug a hole and then built a house in it.» («Our kid» is Manchester and Liverpool talk for a brother, sister or your own child.) Hamza said his 4 out of 10 was a 9; Mum wanted it on the fridge, so «the teacher kept it»; Dad wanted to email the teacher to say well done — and Hamza told the truth before Dad pressed send. Mum said the 4 was fine. The 9 was the only thing that made her sad.
explain: Some lies grow into nonsense. Lucy in Auckland «went to Disneyland», then «met Elsa», then got «her real crown». Theo skipped football for «a cold»; the coach saw him at the cinema, so it was «a cinema for sick people». The coach laughed so hard he forgot to be angry — but Theo was still benched. Wee Callum in Glasgow said he'd scored a hat-trick, and Dad was so chuffed (pleased) that the story reached the club newsletter. «Callum? He was in goal,» said the coach. Dad said: «I'd rather a true save than a fake goal, ya numpty.»
explain: Ava, new at school and wanting friends fast, showed a photo of «her horse» at Grandpa's farm; a girl did a reverse image search and found the photo on a horse-riding website. Sam said his project was at school, then «in the teacher's cupboard», until the teacher said «What project? You never handed one in.» Emily promised a dentist's note «tomorrow» on Monday and again on Tuesday; on Wednesday the school called home.
explain: The moment you notice you are inventing a second lie is the best moment to stop: «Nah, I made that up.» The way out of a hole is to stop digging.
fact: A lie usually needs more lies to protect it, because people ask the next question.
fact: A story that keeps changing is the classic sign of a lie.
fact: Each new lie makes the truth harder and more embarrassing to tell.
fact: Many claims can be checked: team lists online, a phone call to a parent, a reverse image search for a photo.
fact: A reverse image search finds where a picture on the internet came from.
fact: Buying time with lies («I'll bring the note tomorrow») makes the trouble bigger every day.
fact: A lie told to one person can travel far, even into a club newsletter.
fact: When caught, inventing an even bigger story makes things worse, not better.
fact: Parents can help with a bad mark; they cannot help with a faked good one.
fact: You don't need invented things, like a horse or a famous uncle, to be liked.
fact: The earliest moment to stop a lie is the easiest one.
fact: «Wicked» in Boston and New England means «very»; «chuffed» (UK) means pleased; «our kid» (North West England) means a brother, sister or son.
exercise: Why did Noah say his uncle was «injured»? | His mates asked for an autograph | His uncle really had hurt his knee | He wanted to sound kind and caring | The Red Sox had lost a big game that week
exercise: How was Noah's story finally checked? | A mate looked at the team list online | His uncle came into school to say hello | The Red Sox phoned the school office | His mum told the class
exercise: Where was Freya really? | At the park after dark | At Amy's house | At Amy's gran's | At the library
exercise: Why was Freya grounded for two weeks, not one? | One week for the park, one for the lies | Two weeks for missing dinner twice | Because Amy's mum was really angry with her | Because she went to two gran's houses
exercise: What had Jake really done with his bus pass? | Swapped it for sweets | Lost it on the 86 | Left it at school | Given it to his mum
exercise: How did Jake's lie finally fall apart? | Mum saw a girl using a pass with his photo | The bus company found the pass and posted it home | Jake owned up the very next day | A teacher handed in the pass
exercise: How did Ava's made-up horse get found out? | A girl did a reverse image search on the photo | A friend visited Grandpa's farm and saw no horse | Her grandpa told the class | Ava drew the horse badly in art class
exercise: Sam said his project was at school, then in the teacher's cupboard. What did the teacher say? | «What project? You never handed one in» | «Oh yes, I've got it here somewhere» | «It must be in the hall with the others» | «Bring it in tomorrow then»
exercise: Who said «Callum? He was in goal»? | The coach | Uncle Rab, laughing | Callum's dad | Callum's teacher
exercise: Emily kept promising a dentist's note «tomorrow». What happened on Wednesday? | The school called home | The teacher forgot all about it | Emily finally brought a real note | The dentist rang the school
exercise: Why did Hamza tell the truth in the end? | Dad was about to email the teacher to say well done | Mum found the real test in the bottom of his bag | His teacher rang home about the test | His sister told on him
exercise: What made Hamza's mum sad? | The fake 9, not the real 4 | The real 4 out of 10 on the test | That he didn't do any more tests | That Dad wanted to email the school
exercise: Lucy's Disneyland story ended with Elsa giving her what? | Her real crown | A signed photo | A free ride | A magic wand
exercise: Theo was seen at the cinema. What did he say it was? | A cinema for sick people | A doctor's waiting room with a big TV | His cousin's birthday party | A football film for training
exercise: What is a sure sign that someone's story is a lie? | It keeps changing each time it is asked about | It has lots and lots of little details in it | It is told quietly | It is about something that happened at the weekend
exercise: What is the best moment to stop a growing lie? | The moment you notice you need a second lie | Right after the third lie, not before | Only when you are caught out by someone | Never, just keep going
test: Mate in Boston: «that's wicked awesome». What does «wicked» mean there? | Very | Evil | Boring | Scary
test: Mum in Manchester: «Our kid, you dug a hole and then built a house in it.» What does she mean? | Jake made a small problem huge with more lies | Jake would make a really good builder one day | Jake has to dig the garden | Jake lost his bus pass down a hole
test: Kid: «just keep adding stuff, they'll never check». What usually happens? | The bigger the story, the more ways to check it | Bigger stories are always believed by grown-ups | People get bored and stop asking | Adding lots of details makes a lie turn true
test: Theo: «it was a cinema for sick people, coach». Why does this make things worse? | It's a bigger, sillier lie on top of the first | It sounds completely true to the coach | Coaches love stories about cinemas | Sick people really do go to special cinemas
test: Callum's dad was chuffed about the hat-trick. How did he feel? | Pleased and proud | Angry and upset | Bored and tired | Scared and worried
test: What would have happened if Hamza had said «I got a 4» at the start? | His parents would help him practise, with no sad lie | They would have kicked him out of the house | They'd put the 4 on the fridge | Nothing, because nobody cares about tests anyway
test: Ava's new: she wants friends fast. What's better than a made-up horse? | Real things about her, even small ones | A made-up pony instead | A made-up famous uncle who plays football | Saying nothing at all forever
test: Lucy: «and Elsa gave me her REAL crown». Her friend says «yeah nah» and changes the subject. Why is that worse than a fight? | It shows she has stopped believing Lucy | It means she believes every word of it | It means she secretly wants the crown | It means Lucy won
test: Emily: «I'll bring the dentist note tomorrow, promise». What's the wise thing at this point? | Stop and admit there's no note | Promise again for Thursday instead | Write her own note | Say the dentist lost it
test: Teacher's sign on the wall: «If you're in a hole, stop digging.» What does it mean for lies? | Stop adding lies and tell the truth | Never play in the sandpit | Holes are dangerous at school | Keep going until you're out of the hole
test: Freya says Amy's house, then Amy's gran's, then «the other gran». What does the changing story tell Mum? | That Freya is probably lying | That Amy has many grans | That Freya is confused about houses | Nothing at all
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a single falsehood commonly necessitates further falsehoods. | Subsequent questions require additional invented answers | Falsehoods multiply of their own accord over time | Listeners request that further falsehoods be told to them | The first falsehood is always forgotten
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an account which alters upon each successive enquiry? | It is a principal indication that the account is false | It is a principal indication that the account is true | It indicates superior memory on the part of the speaker | It is of no evidential significance
exam: It is given that a pupil reported a mark of nine in place of an actual mark of four. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The parents could assist with the mark of four, but the false report occasioned disappointment | The false mark improved the pupil's understanding of the subject | The parents preferred the false mark, it being the higher of the two and so a cause for pride | The actual mark was of no importance whatever
exam: Determine the most advantageous juncture at which to abandon a falsehood. | Upon first perceiving the need for a further falsehood | Upon the telling of a third falsehood | Solely upon discovery of the falsehood by another person | At no juncture whatever
